    {
      "id": "Dkm_P7zkGrZUFpOznqELD",
      "type": "frame",
      "x": 2350,
      "y": 80,
      "width": 1920,
      "height": 1080,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a4",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 198585994,
      "version": 3,
      "versionNonce": 1612241519,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1795209244330,
      "created": 1795209244330,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "customData": {
        "slidesOrder": 1,
        "mcpSlideLayout": "steps",
        "mcpSlideDesign": {
          "style": "clean",
          "accent": "blue",
          "decor": false
        },
        "presenterNotes": "Getting started — the 0-to-100 of an intelligent loop, built one beat at a time. ① A process worth improving: a failing pipeline that a human babysits by clicking through jobs. ② A skill: plain markdown that says \"given an MR, find its pipeline, report every stage and job with its state\". ③ We notice status-querying is deterministic, so a script moves into the skill folder — fewer tokens, same answer; the model spends tokens only on judgment. ④ An agent: one more markdown file — what it knows, may do, must do — whose initialPrompt is a /loop over the skill, diagnosing failed jobs against a runbook plus the repo and job config. ⑤ Like any algorithm it now has a loop, a goal, accepted states, and a break condition where it asks a human. That's a live, somewhat autonomous loop over a real slice of the SDLC — and every cog in the Armada is built this same way."
      },
      "name": "Getting started"
    },
    {
      "id": "Qsma-o_UoVcKJmOFSr96r",
      "type": "frame",
      "x": 4620,
      "y": 80,
      "width": 1920,
      "height": 1080,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aQ",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 298425738,
      "version": 4,
      "versionNonce": 1784184623,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1795209244528,
      "created": 1795209244528,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "customData": {
        "slidesOrder": 2,
        "mcpSlideLayout": "diagram",
        "mcpSlideDesign": {
          "style": "clean",
          "accent": "blue",
          "decor": false
        },
        "presenterNotes": "SDLC — the line at a glance. The operator files work as tickets (the admiral grooms them to Ready); a captain dispatches them; a worker (or a hand, with you at the keyboard) implements; a sailor lands the MR; reconcile reaps it to Done. When implementation or merge hits something it can't settle, it escalates to TRIAGE — the captain that dispatched the work (planned: today this isn't wired yet). The captain runs the same loop as any other process: if it's fixable, it resolves it and the cog resumes; if not, it goes back to the operator for a human decision."
      },
      "name": "SDLC"
    },
